body:
Deck/library upgrade: `kalide upgrade`, run with no path in the current directory, brings an existing kalide project — a deck directory or a template library directory — forward to the format and scaffold content of the running kalide, offline and without clobbering author work. It is the inverse of the scaffolding commands' refusals (cli-init-refuse-existing, init-library): where init refuses to run because kalide's paths already exist, upgrade runs precisely because they do, and refreshes only the content kalide still owns.

Project detection: the current directory is a deck when it holds `kalide.yaml`, and a template library when it holds `library.yaml`. Holding both, or neither, is an error that names what was found and what was expected, exits non-zero and writes nothing. There is no path argument and no `--force`; the conservative default is the only behaviour.

Scope (two halves): (1) refresh the kalide-owned scaffold files — the embedded `AGENTS.md` author guides and the seed starter content kalide originally wrote — when they are absent or byte-identical to a known released kalide version (see upgrade-refresh-owned-scaffold); and (2) migrate structural/format changes across kalide versions, keyed by the project's format anchors — a library's `library.yaml` `format:` (domain.value.library-manifest) and the format the running binary implements (domain.value.version) — so a future old `library.yaml` format or `template.yaml` schema is brought forward by a registered migration; the registered migration set is empty today (see upgrade-migrate-format). Author content is never written: slide files and frontmatter, section templates, author-authored templates/themes/media, `kalide.yaml` author keys, and any file the author changed are left byte-for-byte untouched and reported.

Because a deck's `kalide.yaml` carries no version field, a deck cannot be keyed off a recorded version: no deck format migration is registered today, so the deck migration path is a no-op and upgrade never infers a deck version from its content (requirements.note.upgrade-deck-vs-library). Upgrade is local-filesystem only — no network, no git, no clone or fetch (global.constraint.upgrade-offline) — and must never leave a project partially migrated or broken (global.constraint.upgrade-never-breaks-project).

AC:
- `kalide upgrade` in a deck directory exits 0 and rewrites only kalide-owned scaffold files that are absent or byte-identical to a known released kalide version; author slides, sections, templates, themes and media are byte-for-byte identical before and after;
- a file that already matches the running kalide's version is left as-is (a no-op), not re-written;
- `kalide upgrade` is idempotent: run twice in a row, the second run finds every refreshable file already current, writes nothing and exits 0;
- `kalide upgrade` in a deck that configures `templates:` does not read or write anything under that library — it is a separate directory, possibly outside the deck, and only the deck's own kalide-owned files are in scope (upgrade-owned-scaffold-scope);
- `kalide upgrade` in a template library directory exits 0 and does the same for the library-owned scaffold, applying the format migrations the library's `format:` calls for;
- a file the author changed is never overwritten: it is skipped and the command still exits 0, with the report naming every skipped path (skipping is expected, not an error);
- there is no `--force` flag;
- a directory holding both `kalide.yaml` and `library.yaml`, or neither, fails non-zero with an error naming what was found and writes nothing;
- `kalide upgrade` performs no network and no git operations (global.constraint.upgrade-offline);
- after upgrade, `kalide start` (deck) or loading the library (library) succeeds on the upgraded project (global.constraint.never-serve-broken-deck).
